A.u17. {"problem": "The deputy cannot do what the change is for. approve_commit requires checks_passed (or wording_only), but check_commit and cite_evidence are owner-only. While the owner is away, a commit that has not been checked yet cannot be approved by the deputy, and the deputy cannot run the check. Also, deliver is owner-only, so the deputy's approval cannot be delivered while the owner is away.", "where": "authoring.domain.yaml, actions check_commit, cite_evidence, deliver vs approve_commit", "kind": "behaviour"}
A.u14. {"problem": "The 'only a person approves' rules (only-a-person-commit-approved and only-a-person-commit-main) and the glossary entry 'approval: A person's decision' name approve_commit as the sole path and do not mention a deputy. Their reasons are now incomplete. The glossary still describes only the owner's decision.", "where": "authoring.domain.yaml, rules only-a-person-commit-approved / only-a-person-commit-main; glossary approval", "kind": "reference"}
A.u13. {"problem": "The permission to approve was widened on an action that is still status: confirmed, with its original interview quote, which says nothing about deputies. The new name_deputy action is only proposed. So a confirmed action now depends on an unconfirmed delegation mechanism. This is an element about who may approve, and the domain's own rule requires a second person for those, yet the change is not marked permission_scope.", "where": "authoring.domain.yaml, actions approve_commit (status/source) and name_deputy (status: proposed)", "kind": "contradiction"}
A.u12. {"problem": "approve_commit now lists the deputy role as an actor, but nothing ties the approver to the deputy the owner named. There is no precondition that actor.id equals target.deputy, so any actor in the deputy role (dan) can approve any commit, including one where no deputy was named. This grants the role standing approval power, not approval on the owner's delegation. name_deputy also takes an unchecked free string, so it can name someone who holds no deputy role.", "where": "authoring.domain.yaml, actions approve_commit (actor, preconditions) and name_deputy (params.deputy)", "kind": "behaviour"}
A.u1. {"problem": "This change widens who may approve a commit, which is an element about who may approve. Existing rules require a second person, never an agent, to confirm such elements (permission_scope, second-person-for-permissions). approve_commit stays status: confirmed even though its actor list and description changed and the new part has no source or quote. name_deputy and the deputy matrix cell are only 'proposed'. The deployed approve_commit therefore allows deputies before the supporting action that names one is confirmed.", "where": "authoring.domain.yaml, actions.approve_commit status/source versus name_deputy status: proposed and the Commit matrix cell", "kind": "contradiction"}
A.u11. {"problem": "No scenario covers the new behaviour: a named deputy approving, an unnamed deputy being denied, or the owner naming a deputy before approval. The only approval scenario uses the owner. The deputy world actor and the deputy role are therefore untested, and the missing check above goes undetected.", "where": "authoring.domain.yaml, scenarios and world.actors.deputy", "kind": "other"}
A.u18. {"problem": "The change widens who may approve, which is a permission-scope element, but it does not follow the existing rule that such elements need two people. approve_commit stays status: confirmed with its old interview quote, which never mentioned a deputy, so the widened actor list is not actually confirmed. The new role and the 'deputy' field are not marked as permission-scope.", "where": "authoring.domain.yaml, approve_commit status/source, confirm_element precondition second-person-for-permissions, role deputy", "kind": "other"}
A.u6. {"problem": "The change widens who may approve, but no scenario covers it: a deputy approving a commit, a deputy being denied when not named, or name_deputy being denied after approval. The only approval scenario is still owner-only. The new permission is untested, and the missing check above would go unnoticed.", "where": "authoring.domain.yaml, scenarios (approve-only-after-a-passing-check and absent deputy scenarios)", "kind": "other"}
A.u2. {"problem": "name_deputy takes a free-text string for the deputy and does not check it against the deputy role. The owner can name anyone, including a non-deputy or an unknown id. The commit's recorded deputy and the actors able to approve it can then differ, and the deputy role is a separate, unlinked grant.", "where": "authoring.domain.yaml, actions.name_deputy params.deputy; Commit.deputy; roles/world.actors.deputy", "kind": "other"}
A.u5. {"problem": "name_deputy takes a free string and stores it in Commit.deputy. The deputy role is a separate concept (roles, world.actors.deputy). The named value is not tied to a person who holds the deputy role, so a commit can name someone who cannot act, and a role-holder can act without being named. The role and the per-commit field are two unlinked ways of expressing the same permission.", "where": "authoring.domain.yaml, actions.name_deputy params.deputy / Commit.deputy / roles", "kind": "other"}
A.u8. {"problem": "The glossary still defines approval only as 'A person's decision', and the rules 'only-a-person-commit-approved' and 'only-a-person-commit-main' cite approve_commit as the sole path. Neither mentions the deputy, so the documentation of who may approve is now incomplete.", "where": "authoring.domain.yaml, glossary.approval and rules.only-a-person-commit-approved", "kind": "reference"}
A.u16. {"problem": "approve_commit now lists the deputy role as an actor but has no precondition tying the actor to the deputy the owner named. The description says 'the deputy the owner named', but anyone in the deputy role (dan) can approve any commit, whether or not a deputy was named on it. name_deputy only stores a free string in Commit.deputy, and nothing ever compares it with actor.id.", "where": "authoring.domain.yaml, action approve_commit (actor, preconditions) and name_deputy / Commit.deputy", "kind": "contradiction"}
A.u3. {"problem": "Approvals can now be made by someone other than the owner, but nothing else was updated to match. There is no scenario covering a deputy approving, an unnamed deputy being denied, or name_deputy being denied after approval. The glossary 'approval' entry and the rule reasons for Commit.approved and Commit.main still describe only the owner's flow. The handoff to Delivery passes approver: actor.id, which can now be a deputy. Delivery's approver role and world list only ana, so that id has no matching role there.", "where": "authoring.domain.yaml scenarios and glossary.approval; handoffs.deliver_approved_commit; delivery.domain.yaml roles/world.actors.approver", "kind": "reference"}
A.u4. {"problem": "approve_commit now lists the deputy role as an actor, but nothing ties the approver to the deputy the owner named. There is no precondition such as actor.id == target.deputy, and no check that a deputy has been named at all. Any actor in the deputy role (dan) can approve any commit, including one where no deputy was named or a different one was. This contradicts the new description 'the deputy the owner named' and the intent of the change request.", "where": "authoring.domain.yaml, actions.approve_commit (actor, preconditions)", "kind": "contradiction"}
A.u10. {"problem": "approve_commit stays status: confirmed and keeps its old interview source ('9번은 추천대로...'), which says nothing about deputies. A permission change to who may approve was made without a new confirmation. name_deputy and the matrix cell for it are only proposed and have no source. The existing rule that elements about who may approve need a second person to confirm is bypassed.", "where": "authoring.domain.yaml, approve_commit status/source; name_deputy; transitions.Commit.recorded.name_deputy", "kind": "other"}
A.u7. {"problem": "This change grants approval rights, so it concerns who may approve. The existing rule says an element about who may approve or deploy needs two people (permission_scope, second_confirm_element). The new approve_commit actor and the new name_deputy action are not marked or handled that way. The deputy approval path is also declared in a way that makes it effectively confirmed without a second person. Separately, name_deputy is only 'proposed' while approve_commit remains 'confirmed' and now depends on the new role.", "where": "authoring.domain.yaml, actions.approve_commit vs confirm_element 'second-person-for-permissions'; name_deputy status", "kind": "contradiction"}
A.u9. {"problem": "approve_commit now lists the deputy role as an actor, but no precondition checks that the actor is the deputy named on this commit. Any actor in the deputy role (dan) can approve any commit, whether or not the owner named them. This contradicts the description ('the deputy the owner named') and name_deputy ('may approve this commit'). The Commit.deputy value set by name_deputy is never read, so naming a deputy has no effect.", "where": "authoring.domain.yaml, action approve_commit (actor, preconditions) and name_deputy (effects)", "kind": "contradiction"}
A.u15. {"problem": "No scenario covers the new behaviour: naming a deputy, a deputy approving, a deputy being denied when none was named, or name_deputy being denied after approval. The only approval scenario uses the owner, so the new permission path is not checked at all.", "where": "authoring.domain.yaml, scenarios (approve-only-after-a-passing-check) and world fixtures", "kind": "other"}
A.u0. {"problem": "approve_commit now lists the deputy role as an actor, but no precondition ties the approver to the deputy the owner named for that commit. Any actor in the deputy role (dan) can approve any commit, including one where no deputy was named or where a different person was named. This contradicts the new description, 'the owner, or the deputy the owner named'. The action also has no check that actor.id equals target.deputy, so the Commit.deputy field is never used.", "where": "authoring.domain.yaml, actions.approve_commit (actor, preconditions) and name_deputy", "kind": "contradiction"}
A.u19. {"problem": "Nothing exercises the new behaviour. No scenario covers naming a deputy, a deputy approving, or a deputy being denied. The glossary entries for 'approval' and the description of the owner as the one who approves are not updated. Authoring's interface changes (a new role and a new action), but the composition still pins interface 1 and Delivery's roles and approver data know nothing about a deputy approver.", "where": "authoring.domain.yaml scenarios/glossary; toprope-self.composition.yaml members.authoring.interface", "kind": "reference"}
